WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:06.625
This is Part 1 of a two-part narrated
tour of a Federal Modernization Workbook

3
00:00:06.625 --> 00:00:10.472
evaluation: Cloud DNS on
AWS, Azure and Oracle Cloud,

4
00:00:10.472 --> 00:00:13.587
with native resolvers, Infoblox and BIND.

5
00:00:13.587 --> 00:00:18.500
It's research under review, written
for FedRAMP Moderate civilian agencies.

6
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:24.412
Part 1 covers the three native resolvers
in depth: the Route 53 VPC Resolver,

7
00:00:24.412 --> 00:00:29.562
the Azure DNS Private Resolver,
and the Oracle Cloud VCN resolver.

8
00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:32.838
For each: its endpoints
and forwarding rules,

9
00:00:32.838 --> 00:00:35.937
its query logging and
its DNS-layer filter.

10
00:00:35.937 --> 00:00:39.305
Then what the providers say
about their government clouds,

11
00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:41.436
and how all three meet on-premises DNS.

12
00:00:41.436 --> 00:00:45.490
Statements marked design guidance
are the program's recommendations,

13
00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:50.240
inferences are labeled, and confirm marks
a point to settle with the provider.

14
00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:56.840
Why does cloud DNS need a deck of its own?

15
00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:01.177
Book 08 of the workbook asks
for one authoritative policy

16
00:01:01.177 --> 00:01:05.152
and state plane that every
other DNS system consumes.

17
00:01:05.152 --> 00:01:10.028
Cloud DNS services are synchronized
targets or forwarding destinations,

18
00:01:10.028 --> 00:01:12.465
not independently authored sources.

19
00:01:12.465 --> 00:01:15.940
The warning is about authoring
copies in every silo.

20
00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:21.476
That produces split-brain answers, names
that work in one cloud but not another,

21
00:01:21.476 --> 00:01:24.202
and uneven Protective DNS and logging.

22
00:01:24.202 --> 00:01:28.477
The workbook already covers
Infoblox with Azure and AWS.

23
00:01:28.477 --> 00:01:31.308
It covered the Route 53 Resolver thinly,

24
00:01:31.308 --> 00:01:36.453
and Oracle Cloud only at the comparison
level, so Part 1 adds both in depth.

25
00:01:36.453 --> 00:01:40.250
BIND in a cloud wasn't
covered at all; that's Part 2.

26
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.650
Here is the answer in brief.

27
00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:49.719
Use the native resolver as the
forwarding bridge in each cloud,

28
00:01:49.719 --> 00:01:51.988
and keep the policy in the DDI plane.

29
00:01:51.988 --> 00:01:57.023
The Route 53 VPC Resolver and the
Azure DNS Private Resolver are managed,

30
00:01:57.023 --> 00:01:59.626
zone-redundant forwarding services,

31
00:01:59.626 --> 00:02:04.662
and both providers recommend them
over DNS servers on virtual machines.

32
00:02:04.662 --> 00:02:08.187
Oracle Cloud gives every
virtual cloud network,

33
00:02:08.187 --> 00:02:11.800
or VCN, a dedicated resolver
that does the same job.

34
00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:16.197
None of the three is an authority
for the agency's own namespaces,

35
00:02:16.197 --> 00:02:19.350
and neither AWS nor Azure
signs private zones.

36
00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:24.983
Oracle Cloud is the one with no native
DNS-layer filter for private DNS, so in a

37
00:02:24.983 --> 00:02:31.063
VCN the agency's policy applies only if
queries are forwarded to agency resolvers.

38
00:02:31.063 --> 00:02:32.724
And, as design guidance,

39
00:02:32.724 --> 00:02:36.924
run your own DNS servers in a
cloud only for a stated reason.

40
00:02:36.924 --> 00:02:38.680
Part 2 covers that.

41
00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.017
Start with AWS.

42
00:02:44.017 --> 00:02:48.641
AWS renamed the Route 53
Resolver the Route 53 VPC

43
00:02:48.641 --> 00:02:53.717
Resolver when it introduced
Route 53 Global Resolver.

44
00:02:53.717 --> 00:02:58.818
Several AWS pages still use the older
name, so both appear in runbooks.

45
00:02:58.818 --> 00:03:05.068
Every VPC reaches it at the VPC's base
address plus two, called VPC plus two.

46
00:03:05.068 --> 00:03:09.793
It answers the VPC's own EC2 names
and records in private hosted

47
00:03:09.793 --> 00:03:14.518
zones associated with the VPC,
and it recurses for public names.

48
00:03:14.518 --> 00:03:15.881
One quota matters.

49
00:03:15.881 --> 00:03:20.397
Each network interface may
send 1,024 packets per second

50
00:03:20.397 --> 00:03:24.818
to link-local services, shared
by DNS and other services.

51
00:03:24.818 --> 00:03:28.006
It can't be raised, and
traffic over it is rejected.

52
00:03:28.006 --> 00:03:33.917
That matters to any DNS server on
EC2 that forwards to VPC plus two.

53
00:03:33.917 --> 00:03:39.316
Route 53 Global Resolver is a different
thing, an internet-reachable resolver for

54
00:03:39.316 --> 00:03:45.150
branches and on-premises sites, listed in
30 commercial Regions and not in GovCloud.

55
00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:53.212
Hybrid resolution in AWS runs
through two kinds of endpoint.

56
00:03:53.212 --> 00:03:57.239
An inbound endpoint lets outside
resolvers forward queries

57
00:03:57.239 --> 00:04:01.187
into a VPC and get the answers
it sees, private zones included.

58
00:04:01.187 --> 00:04:05.126
It has at least two addresses,
in different Availability Zones.

59
00:04:05.126 --> 00:04:09.482
Each address handles up to
10,000 UDP queries per second,

60
00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:12.987
but as few as 1,500 under
connection tracking.

61
00:04:12.987 --> 00:04:17.206
It speaks plain DNS, DNS over HTTPS,

62
00:04:17.206 --> 00:04:23.837
and a FIPS 140-2 version of
DNS over HTTPS, inbound only.

63
00:04:23.837 --> 00:04:29.175
A second type, the delegation inbound
endpoint, answers NS delegations.

64
00:04:29.175 --> 00:04:33.094
An outbound endpoint goes the
other way: it sends queries that

65
00:04:33.094 --> 00:04:37.013
match a forwarding rule from the
VPC to the agency's resolvers.

66
00:04:37.013 --> 00:04:44.438
It speaks only plain DNS and DNS over
HTTPS, so there's no FIPS option outbound.

67
00:04:44.438 --> 00:04:47.810
And sharing a rule shares
its outbound endpoint too.

68
00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:53.510
Rules decide where queries go.

69
00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:57.247
A forwarding rule names one
domain and its target addresses.

70
00:04:57.247 --> 00:04:59.827
The most specific matching rule wins,

71
00:04:59.827 --> 00:05:03.822
and a rule overrides a private
hosted zone of the same name.

72
00:05:03.822 --> 00:05:08.097
A rule for the root, a single
dot, forwards almost everything.

73
00:05:08.097 --> 00:05:11.869
System rules carve a subdomain
back out of a forwarding rule,

74
00:05:11.869 --> 00:05:16.547
so it resolves locally, and delegation
rules handle delegated subdomains.

75
00:05:16.547 --> 00:05:20.135
Rules are shared through
AWS Resource Access Manager.

76
00:05:20.135 --> 00:05:25.247
The receiving account associates them with
its VPCs but can't change or delete them.

77
00:05:25.247 --> 00:05:28.415
Profiles go further,
bundling zones, rules,

78
00:05:28.415 --> 00:05:33.122
firewall rule groups and logging
settings, one Profile per VPC.

79
00:05:33.122 --> 00:05:39.289
Private hosted zones answer only inside
associated VPCs, up to 300 per zone,

80
00:05:39.289 --> 00:05:45.110
and an empty match returns NXDOMAIN,
with no fallback to public DNS.

81
00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:53.698
DNS Firewall applies rule groups
of allow, alert and block rules.

82
00:05:53.698 --> 00:05:59.798
It uses custom or AWS managed domain lists,
and the managed lists can't be browsed.

83
00:05:59.798 --> 00:06:05.248
The Advanced tier detects domain
generation algorithms and DNS tunneling.

84
00:06:05.248 --> 00:06:09.997
It filters only queries that pass
through the VPC Resolver, including those

85
00:06:09.997 --> 00:06:15.123
arriving through inbound endpoints, and by
default it fails closed, with SERVFAIL.

86
00:06:15.123 --> 00:06:20.057
Query logging records queries from
the VPC, through the endpoints,

87
00:06:20.057 --> 00:06:24.184
and firewall actions, to
CloudWatch Logs, S3 or Firehose.

88
00:06:24.184 --> 00:06:29.485
It logs only queries that reach the VPC
Resolver, and not answers from its cache.

89
00:06:29.485 --> 00:06:35.260
DNSSEC: the VPC Resolver can validate
public signed names, enabled per VPC.

90
00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:38.460
Route 53 signs public hosted zones only.

91
00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:41.768
And the VPC Resolver
returns no DNSSEC records,

92
00:06:41.768 --> 00:06:45.160
so a resolver behind it
can't validate for itself.

93
00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:49.850
AWS warns that enabling validation
could result in an outage.

94
00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:55.213
What changes in GovCloud?

95
00:06:55.213 --> 00:07:00.674
AWS's GovCloud page for Route 53 says
public and private DNS work in GovCloud

96
00:07:00.674 --> 00:07:04.141
US-West and US-East, the
control plane is in US-West,

97
00:07:04.141 --> 00:07:09.775
and public hosted zone queries are
answered from within the FedRAMP boundary.

98
00:07:09.775 --> 00:07:12.997
That page doesn't mention
the Resolver features,

99
00:07:12.997 --> 00:07:15.413
so the table draws on other AWS pages.

100
00:07:15.413 --> 00:07:17.838
Endpoints and rules: yes.

101
00:07:17.838 --> 00:07:23.208
Resolver delegation is the conflict: the
GovCloud guide says it's not available,

102
00:07:23.208 --> 00:07:25.975
and an April 2026 announcement says it is.

103
00:07:25.975 --> 00:07:26.862
Confirm.

104
00:07:26.862 --> 00:07:30.324
DNS Firewall and its managed lists: yes.

105
00:07:30.324 --> 00:07:32.275
Of the Advanced protections,

106
00:07:32.275 --> 00:07:36.663
only dictionary DGA was announced
for GovCloud; confirm the rest.

107
00:07:36.663 --> 00:07:39.450
Query logging and Profiles: yes.

108
00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:43.913
Signing of public zones: yes,
with the key in US-West.

109
00:07:43.913 --> 00:07:48.287
Validation in the VPC Resolver
isn't stated, so confirm.

110
00:07:48.287 --> 00:07:50.920
And Global Resolver isn't listed.

111
00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:55.645
Now Azure.

112
00:07:55.645 --> 00:08:00.696
Book 08's Chapter 07 covers the Private
Resolver's hub-and-spoke design,

113
00:08:00.696 --> 00:08:03.657
so here is the summary
with today's limits.

114
00:08:03.657 --> 00:08:08.177
The inbound endpoint receives queries
from on premises or other clouds

115
00:08:08.177 --> 00:08:12.245
and answers from private DNS zones
linked to its virtual network.

116
00:08:12.245 --> 00:08:16.645
There can be five per resolver, at
10,000 queries per second each.

117
00:08:16.645 --> 00:08:22.213
The outbound endpoint and its forwarding
ruleset send queries for named domains to

118
00:08:22.213 --> 00:08:25.315
target servers; a ruleset
holds up to 1,000 rules,

119
00:08:25.315 --> 00:08:28.258
and a virtual network
links only one ruleset.

120
00:08:28.258 --> 00:08:32.099
It's a single-region service,
zone-redundant inside the region,

121
00:08:32.099 --> 00:08:37.295
and it doesn't work in encrypted virtual
networks or IPv6-enabled subnets.

122
00:08:37.295 --> 00:08:40.400
And Azure DNS doesn't
support zone transfers,

123
00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:45.870
so a BIND server can't be a secondary of
an Azure private zone, or the reverse.

124
00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:54.520
Azure's DNS-layer filter
is DNS resolver policy.

125
00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:59.682
Microsoft renamed it from DNS security
policy, so expect both names.

126
00:08:59.682 --> 00:09:04.916
A resolver policy filters and logs
every query in a linked virtual network,

127
00:09:04.916 --> 00:09:08.546
public and private, with
allow, block and alert rules.

128
00:09:08.546 --> 00:09:11.584
It includes a Microsoft-managed
threat intelligence

129
00:09:11.584 --> 00:09:14.420
list from the Microsoft
Security Response Center.

130
00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:18.516
Its logs go to Log Analytics,
in the DNS query logs table,

131
00:09:18.516 --> 00:09:22.795
or to storage or Event Hubs,
through diagnostic settings.

132
00:09:22.795 --> 00:09:26.688
Microsoft lists resource logs
only for resolver policies,

133
00:09:26.688 --> 00:09:31.057
so the inference is that per-query
logs come from a resolver policy,

134
00:09:31.057 --> 00:09:34.870
or Azure Firewall's DNS proxy,
not from the resolver itself.

135
00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:37.314
DNSSEC: Azure can sign public zones,

136
00:09:37.314 --> 00:09:40.770
but the Azure-provided
resolver doesn't validate.

137
00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:44.036
No Microsoft page describes
signing private zones,

138
00:09:44.036 --> 00:09:48.120
or whether the Private Resolver
validates, so confirm that.

139
00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:54.576
Oracle Cloud Infrastructure matters here

140
00:09:54.576 --> 00:09:57.357
because the HR platform
runs on Oracle Cloud.

141
00:09:57.357 --> 00:10:06.232
Every VCN gets a dedicated resolver,
listening on 169.254.169.254.

142
00:10:06.232 --> 00:10:10.009
It answers from the private
views attached to it, in order,

143
00:10:10.009 --> 00:10:14.269
then its default view, then its
resolver rules, then the internet.

144
00:10:14.269 --> 00:10:16.877
Oracle says it performs recursion to the

145
00:10:16.877 --> 00:10:20.020
internet and DNSSEC
validation for signed zones.

146
00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:21.607
The trap is in that order.

147
00:10:21.607 --> 00:10:25.470
A match in the sequence ends
resolution, even an NXDOMAIN.

148
00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:28.541
So a private zone for
example dot com hides a

149
00:10:28.541 --> 00:10:31.868
forwarding rule for onprem
dot example dot com.

150
00:10:31.868 --> 00:10:34.683
Views can be attached to many resolvers,

151
00:10:34.683 --> 00:10:38.095
sharing private DNS data
across VCNs in a region.

152
00:10:38.095 --> 00:10:42.632
Same-name private and public
zones give split-horizon DNS.

153
00:10:42.632 --> 00:10:48.300
Between VCNs, resolution uses resolver
endpoints over a peering gateway.

154
00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:56.750
A listening endpoint receives queries
from on premises or other VCNs.

155
00:10:56.750 --> 00:11:01.199
A forwarding endpoint sends
queries no view answers to targets,

156
00:11:01.199 --> 00:11:03.213
under up to 50 resolver rules.

157
00:11:03.213 --> 00:11:08.788
A rule matches a client address block, a
domain, or None, which matches any query.

158
00:11:08.788 --> 00:11:11.921
Forward is the only action,
the first match wins,

159
00:11:11.921 --> 00:11:14.813
and an unreachable target ends processing.

160
00:11:14.813 --> 00:11:20.624
A subnet's DHCP options can name a custom
resolver of up to three DNS servers.

161
00:11:20.624 --> 00:11:25.540
Those instances reach private views and

162
00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:30.600
rules only if 169.254.169.254
is in that list.

163
00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:31.713
Then the gaps.

164
00:11:31.713 --> 00:11:34.615
There's no native DNS-layer policy:

165
00:11:34.615 --> 00:11:39.800
no Oracle page describes a DNS
firewall for the VCN resolver.

166
00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:43.421
Private resolver logs, with
each query's resolution path,

167
00:11:43.421 --> 00:11:46.425
are documented for the
commercial realms only.

168
00:11:46.425 --> 00:11:50.670
And the resolver can serve stale
answers for up to 24 hours.

169
00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:56.033
Who authorizes all this?

170
00:11:56.033 --> 00:12:02.574
AWS's services-in-scope page, updated
October fifteenth, 2026, lists one row,

171
00:12:02.574 --> 00:12:08.150
Amazon Route 53, in scope for
FedRAMP Class C, formerly Moderate,

172
00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:13.083
in US East and West, and Class
D, formerly High, in GovCloud.

173
00:12:13.083 --> 00:12:16.125
There are no separate rows
for the Resolver features;

174
00:12:16.125 --> 00:12:19.582
generally available features
are in scope unless excluded.

175
00:12:19.582 --> 00:12:24.644
For a Moderate civilian workload,
both locations cover the DNS service.

176
00:12:24.644 --> 00:12:27.970
As an inference, note that
in the system security plan.

177
00:12:27.970 --> 00:12:32.751
Microsoft's audit-scope page has one
DNS row, at FedRAMP High in Azure and in

178
00:12:32.751 --> 00:12:38.158
Azure Government, with no separate row for
the Private Resolver or resolver policy.

179
00:12:38.158 --> 00:12:41.796
No Learn page states their
Azure Government availability.

180
00:12:41.796 --> 00:12:45.538
The price list carries their
meters in the US Gov zones:

181
00:12:45.538 --> 00:12:48.782
a strong signal, not an
availability statement.

182
00:12:48.782 --> 00:12:50.900
Confirm in the target region.

183
00:12:54.650 --> 00:12:57.163
Oracle's government
cloud works differently.

184
00:12:57.163 --> 00:13:02.239
The US Government Cloud is its own
realm, called OC2, with US Gov East in

185
00:13:02.239 --> 00:13:07.850
Ashburn and US Gov West in Phoenix, and
it holds a FedRAMP High authorization.

186
00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:11.657
And its DNS API is for private DNS only;

187
00:13:11.657 --> 00:13:16.938
public DNS isn't available
in the government realms.

188
00:13:16.938 --> 00:13:20.277
The FedRAMP Marketplace lists
the Government Cloud at High,

189
00:13:20.277 --> 00:13:23.150
and no commercial Oracle
infrastructure offering.

190
00:13:23.150 --> 00:13:26.125
Oracle's software-as-a-service
has its own listings,

191
00:13:26.125 --> 00:13:28.038
such as Fusion Cloud at Moderate.

192
00:13:28.038 --> 00:13:29.513
So, as inferences.

193
00:13:29.513 --> 00:13:32.465
A Moderate system on OCI
infrastructure inherits

194
00:13:32.465 --> 00:13:35.276
from the Government
Cloud's High authorization.

195
00:13:35.276 --> 00:13:39.811
Commercial OCI regions have no FedRAMP
listing of their own, so a federal

196
00:13:39.811 --> 00:13:44.725
system placed there needs its own
authorization basis, settled before design.

197
00:13:44.725 --> 00:13:47.661
Whether private resolver
logging exists in the

198
00:13:47.661 --> 00:13:51.037
government realm isn't
documented; confirm with Oracle.

199
00:13:51.037 --> 00:13:54.181
And an agency holding
only the HR platform's

200
00:13:54.181 --> 00:13:57.250
software-as-a-service has no VCN at all.

201
00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:03.850
How do the three meet on-premises DNS?

202
00:14:03.850 --> 00:14:09.912
The same way, and Book 08's Decision 3
already holds the rules, as design guidance.

203
00:14:09.912 --> 00:14:14.111
Into the cloud: on-premises resolvers,
and other clouds' resolvers,

204
00:14:14.111 --> 00:14:19.165
conditionally forward each cloud namespace
to that cloud's inbound endpoint,

205
00:14:19.165 --> 00:14:21.575
using static addresses written once.

206
00:14:21.575 --> 00:14:25.325
On Oracle Cloud, the entry
point is a listening endpoint.

207
00:14:25.325 --> 00:14:31.275
Neither the AWS nor the Azure inbound
endpoint accepts an ordinary NS delegation

208
00:14:31.275 --> 00:14:37.050
of a private zone; AWS's delegation inbound
endpoint is the exception, and it's new.

209
00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:40.502
Out of the cloud: each
cloud forwards the agency's

210
00:14:40.502 --> 00:14:44.300
namespaces back to the agency
resolvers, by its rules.

211
00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:47.848
On AWS, create the rules once
in a network account and

212
00:14:47.848 --> 00:14:51.725
share them through Resource
Access Manager or a Profile.

213
00:14:51.725 --> 00:14:54.163
Spokes associate them and can't edit them.

214
00:14:54.163 --> 00:14:57.480
That's the cloud form
of one policy object.

215
00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:03.368
First, internet-bound queries.

216
00:15:03.368 --> 00:15:08.337
CISA requires Protective DNS
for all egress DNS resolution,

217
00:15:08.337 --> 00:15:14.280
including from agency cloud deployments,
directly or through agency DNS.

218
00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:20.168
None of the native resolvers forwards the
default path to Protective DNS on its own.

219
00:15:20.168 --> 00:15:24.617
That takes a root rule in AWS or
Azure, or a rule whose condition

220
00:15:24.617 --> 00:15:28.718
is None in Oracle Cloud, pointing
at the agency resolvers.

221
00:15:28.718 --> 00:15:31.452
Whether cloud egress
already reaches it some

222
00:15:31.452 --> 00:15:34.330
other way is for the agency
to confirm with CISA.

223
00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:35.730
Second, policy.

224
00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:38.378
The clouds' own filters
are a second policy

225
00:15:38.378 --> 00:15:41.555
surface beside the agency's
response policy zones.

226
00:15:41.555 --> 00:15:45.928
The design guidance: feed them
from the same indicator decisions,

227
00:15:45.928 --> 00:15:50.867
or use them as a backstop, so a block on
premises is also a block in each cloud.

228
00:15:50.867 --> 00:15:54.001
Oracle has no such filter for private DNS,

229
00:15:54.001 --> 00:15:58.793
so its catch-all rule is the only
way agency policy reaches a VCN.

230
00:15:58.793 --> 00:16:01.917
Third, logs: all three go to the SIEM,

231
00:16:01.917 --> 00:16:06.780
parsed through ASIM's DNS
schema, as Book 12 does.

232
00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:14.930
To close Part 1, here's what the providers
don't settle, so confirm it with them.

233
00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:19.893
AWS: Resolver delegation and
DNSSEC validation in GovCloud.

234
00:16:19.893 --> 00:16:24.253
Azure: the Private Resolver's and
resolver policy's availability in each

235
00:16:24.253 --> 00:16:28.904
Azure Government region, and whether
the Private Resolver validates DNSSEC.

236
00:16:28.904 --> 00:16:32.510
Oracle: private resolver
logging in the government realm,

237
00:16:32.510 --> 00:16:36.942
and whether the VCN resolver passes
DNSSEC data to a resolver behind it.

238
00:16:36.942 --> 00:16:42.780
And CISA: whether Protective DNS returns
DNSSEC data to agency forwarders.

239
00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:46.692
To prove the page, the plan is
to build the pattern in test

240
00:16:46.692 --> 00:16:50.605
accounts and run Book 08's
Chapter 10 validation matrix.

241
00:16:50.605 --> 00:16:54.308
Part 2 picks up from here: when
to run your own DNS servers,

242
00:16:54.308 --> 00:16:57.930
BIND, the alignment checklist,
and the federal mapping.

243
00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:02.855
This is research under review, not an
endorsement by any federal agency.

244
00:17:02.855 --> 00:17:03.690
Thank you.
